     Include the auth-missing fallback block with connect instructions. -->

## 2. Scope

Restate the ticket in one sentence; list what is out of scope. Cannot restate it: ask on the ticket ({{COMMENT_COMMAND_SHORT}}), report, stop. Guessing scope wastes the whole run.

## 3. Claim

{{CLAIM_COMMANDS}}
<!-- Status → {{STARTED_STATE}}, self-assign. Prevents parallel sessions double-taking. -->

## 4. Branch

Off freshly-pulled `{{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}`: `<type>/{{TICKET_REF_PATTERN}}-<slug>` (types per `rules/git-workflow.md`). With `--worktree`, or when other tickets are in flight:

```bash
git worktree add ../{{REPO_NAME}}-<ticket-ref> -b <branch> {{DEFAULT_BRANCH}}
```

and work inside it. One worktree per ticket is what makes parallel sessions safe.

## 5. Discover, plan, implement

Follow `rules/workflow.md` steps 2-4: read the docs map's relevant `llm/` and `refs/` entries, reuse before building, write a short plan for non-trivial work{{PLAN_SKILL_NOTE}}, implement only the ticket scope.

## 6. Verify

`/verify` until clean; fix honestly (no skipped tests, no loosened assertions to pass). Stuck after ~3 attempts on one approach: stop, write why, re-plan{{REVIEW_STEP_NOTE}}.

## 7. Update docs that drifted

New pattern, module surface, or flow change → update `llm/PATTERNS.md` / `refs/` / `llm/USER_FLOWS.md` in this same change (arbiter: `rules/llm-docs.md`). This is why the next session can trust the docs.

## 8. Land

Commit per `rules/git-workflow.md` (trailer: `{{COMMIT_TRAILER_EXAMPLE}}`), push, then `/create-pr` (raw `gh pr create` is hook-blocked). Then advance the ticket:

{{REVIEW_TRANSITION_COMMANDS}}
<!-- Status → {{REVIEW_STATE}}, attach PR link per the ticketing reference. -->

## 9. Report

```
## {{KEY_EXAMPLE_SHORT}}: <one-line restatement>
**Changed**: <files/areas, 2-4 lines>
**Verified**: <gate results, actual output not "should work">
**PR**: <url>
**Assumptions**: <or "none">
**Out of scope, worth a ticket**: <or "none">
```

## If blocked

Say exactly where and what would unblock; move the ticket back or comment; never claim done. A truthful stuck report keeps this skill trustworthy enough to run unattended.
